{
  "image_name": "1daf1b6342453cb788c700dbca870d7b.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure compares two forms of pulmonary aspergillosis in the setting of severe viral lung infection. Across the center, fungal conidia and hyphae are shown in the alveolar space above the alveolar epithelium, with macrophages and neutrophils as the main host cells. The left side, labeled CAPA, emphasizes a later ICU presentation associated with corticosteroid use, prominent neutrophil extracellular trap formation, and fungal invasion into lung tissue. Neutrophils are shown releasing NETs around hyphae, suggesting an intense but possibly damaging inflammatory response.\n\nThe right side, labeled IAPA, depicts an earlier ICU presentation with reduced interferon signaling, impaired neutrophil activity, and more aggressive fungal growth. Here the hyphae appear denser and extend through the alveolar barrier into the interstitial space and then into the blood vessel, highlighting angioinvasion. This suggests a shift from mainly tissue invasion in CAPA to deeper vascular invasion in IAPA.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that aspergillosis complicating viral pneumonia can follow different immune-pathology patterns depending on the viral context, with differences in timing, neutrophil behavior, and the extent of fungal spread through the lung and vasculature.",
